in the last half hour u.s secretary of  state mike pompeo has declared al-qaeda  has established a new home base  in iran he made that assertion without  any hard evidence  in what appears to be a last-minute  offensive against tehran as pompeo and  the rest of the trump administration  prepare to leave power in washington  he also said he could confirm the death  of al-qaeda's abu mohammed al-masri  that death was reported last year a  master gunned down by israeli operatives  in al qaeda  has a new home base it is the islamic  republic  of iran as a result  bin laden's wicked creation is poised to  gain strength  and capabilities we ignore this  iran al-qaeda nexus at our own peril  we need to acknowledge it we must  confront it indeed we must  defeat it so let's start with alan  fisher  at the white house allen we were talking  last hour about things that  usually happened in the last week of an  administration  uh how does this sort of sit in that  usual  time frame  it's certainly not unheard of but it is  rather  unusual that the secretary of state  would make such a speech and that he  would  declassify information during a speech  drawing links between al qaeda and iran  just by contrast the these rumors these  stories  these allegations have been around for  some considerable time over a number of  years  even before 9 11 there was talk of iran  having connections with al qaeda  certainly the intelligence community has  always been very skeptical of that  idea mike pompeo has always been keen to  demonize  iran he's very much behind donald  trump's idea of maximum pressure he is  of course  a former cia director so he will get  intelligence across  his desk that some in the intelligence  community won't even see  but we know that the the center for  strategic and international studies very  important think tank here in the united  states  at the end of 2019 published a report  that suggested  the links between al qaeda and iraq iran  are very very small indeed and suggested  that if there were  high-profile members of al qaeda there  was maybe no more than  five living in iran that really doesn't  matter to mike pompeo of course he will  suggest that if you've got one you've  got more than enough  there's a concern that mike pompeo is  doing two things first of all making it  very difficult for the biden  administration the incoming biden  administration  to make any sort of peace moves towards  iran  for any sort of approach to to make sure  that the two nations get on better  and perhaps even consider going back  into the iran nuclear deal which was  negotiated  by joe biden's old boss barack obama's  administration  the second thing that some people on the  hill are very concerned about  is that by tying al qaeda to iran then  mike pompeo and donald trump and the  administration would then have an excuse  to launch some sort of attack  in iran itself now to go and do that  sort of thing you would normally need  congressional approval  given that the house is controlled by  democrats given that the senate is now  controlled by democrats you're not going  to get that however  there is an ongoing authorization for  the use of military force  which targets al-qaeda so if you could  go and say  well we weren't attacking iran we were  attacking members of al qaeda  that is why we carried out that attack  that would certainly give them that and  we know  that there's been some concern  particularly since the turn of the year  that donald trump might be planning some  sort of assault on iran  there's nothing really concrete to back  that up just  some grumbling some chatter in the  intelligence community  annoying fear for some but uh  that would certainly be a a way to  explain  any action that they take if they say  well there is  this authorization for the use of  military force against al-qaeda that is  why we launched that assault not saying  that anything like that is going to  happen  but that is in a way trying to put  that pompeo speech in some sort of  context and so why say all of this now  as we point out so close to the end of  the  administration is this i mean one of the  reasons maybe another attempt to pull  some focus away from everything else  that's going on  well two reasons for it one uh he's not  got the platform to do this much  more often and as i say he's trying to  make things difficult for the incoming  biden administration to make any sort of  moves towards  iran which has been really a country  that has  been targeted by the trump  administration from the very early days  remember back in 2016 before the  election then  donald trump said that one of the first  things he would do on day one would  pull out of the iran nuclear deal he was  talked out of that in a couple of  occasions when he had to  reauthorize but when it came to doing it  for the third time he said i'm  absolutely not doing it  and and refused to do it so pulled the  plug on it at that point  the other thing is mike pompeo is  interested in running for the  presidential nomination for the  republican party in 2024  so being tough in iran he believes plays  well to the base and makes him look  strong  and certainly if you continue to attack  iran  it plays well with conservative base  america has scar tissue from what  happened  during the carter administration when  the hostages were taken  at the iranian embassy they do not trust  iran  that has been the case for many many  many years donald trump to a degree  tapped into that rather than played up  on it he tapped into that and so  there is always a concern about what  iran is going to do next  and in some cases people close to donald  trump would say they've been justified  in in some of the things that iran has  done and they've been it's been true  with  with what they've predicted and what  they've said but they would like to  continue  this campaign of maximum pressure  and they're hoping that by highlighting  this it makes it much more difficult for  joe biden to step away from that  alan fisher's at the white house thank  you for that alan  uh reaction from iran with our  correspondent in the moment i just want  to show you this tweet first of all  javid zarif  we'll go into this in more detail with  dawson jabar in a moment but you know  lines there about warmongering lies  no one has fooled all 911 terrorists  came from secretary pompeo's favorite  middle east destinations none from iran  that's the  iranian foreign minister dulce jabari i  mean  predictable sort of reaction from the  iranians  there but mike pompeo came out with a  lot of  well he came out with a lot of specifics  without necessarily  backing it up with evidence  yes certainly he said that he had  documentation that provided uh  proof that the nine eight of the 9 11  hijackers had traveled to  or through iran prior to the attack that  was carried out on september 11  in 2001 but he didn't specify  what purpose they had for traveling  through this country  he also said that very she believed very  strongly that they have set up al qaeda  has set up an office  base in iran and that they are  continuing their fundraising activities  and he put forth some very very  um distinct and very strong  possibilities or scenarios that if  there was a strong relationship between  this government or the system in iran  and al-qaeda  the combination could be very very uh  deadly for the international community  that they should not let that happen  this kind of language this kind of  rhetoric that was used by  mike pompeo reminded me of when  colin powell was speaking at the u.n in  2003  before the united states invaded iraq it  seemed like there was a groundwork of  being laid  for an excuse to carry out some kind of  an action  this is of course something that we've  heard uh like alan mentioned there was  there's been rumors of this whispers of  this over the past few months that  president trump  wanted to strike some of iran's nuclear  facilities in order to deter  the country from pursuing any further  its nuclear activities but  iran for its part has been very very uh  strong in their position they haven't uh  altered at  all we heard from the supreme leader on  friday who said that  the iranians aren't waiting for the u.s  to return to the jcpoa or the nuclear  agreement of 2015.  what they want is an immediate lifting  of the sanctions that the  u.s has imposed on iran since they  withdrew from the deal in 2018.  they've left it that's fine and we don't  care what is the feeling  but the sanctions they've imposed on us  since then  are absolutely inhumane and have had  really  serious economic consequences on the  population of this country  so that is the position here and this  speech  will be seen as another attempt by this  administration in the final days  to try and uh make things very very  difficult for  president-elect joe biden who of course  has had a personal relationship  of about 30 years with foreign minister  mohammad javad zarif  the foreign minister was the iranian  ambassador to the un  uh decades ago at the same time joe  biden  was also working for the us  within the un so the two have had  numerous meetings they have  had some kind of a relationship in the  past and the foreign minister javad  zarif has spoken about that relationship  and he said that  he believes the president elect to be  much more uh  logical and much more well versed in  foreign policy and  understanding this region so there is a  sense that the incoming administration  will see things very very differently  and certainly this language that we  heard  uh today will not be heard in the coming  weeks after  january 20th thank you dolce jabari in  tehran
 

